 one hundred thousand times per frame. The GPU itself could handle the triangles easily. It's the CPU-to-GPU communication overhead that kills you. Each draw call has setup cost: bind the geometry buffer, bind the material, set the uniforms, issue the draw command. At 60fps that's six million draw calls per second. Your frame rate drops to single digits.

InstancedMesh fixes this. One geometry, one material, N transforms. One draw call. The GPU receives the geometry once and draws it N times, each time with a different position/rotation/scale. The CPU sends one command instead of N. That's the entire trick -- and it changes what's possible in the browser.

## The InstancedMesh basics

The constructor takes three arguments: a geometry, a material, and a count (how many instances). Then you set each instance's transform using a Matrix4:

```javascript
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x080810);

const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(
  60, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 200
);
camera.position.set(0, 15, 30);

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
renderer.setPixelRatio(window.devicePixelRatio);
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

const controls = new OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);
controls.enableDamping = true;

scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x222244, 1.0));
const sun = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffeedd, 2.0);
sun.position.set(5, 10, 5);
scene.add(sun);

// one geometry, one material, 10000 instances
const count = 10000;
const geo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.3, 0.3, 0.3);
const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0x6688cc,
  roughness: 0.5
});

const mesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geo, mat, count);

// place each instance with a matrix
const dummy = new THREE.Object3D();

for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  dummy.position.set(
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40,
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40,
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40
  );
  dummy.rotation.set(
    Math.random() * Math.PI * 2,
    Math.random() * Math.PI * 2,
    Math.random() * Math.PI * 2
  );
  dummy.scale.setScalar(0.5 + Math.random() * 1.5);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  mesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
}

scene.add(mesh);

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  controls.update();
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}

animate();

window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  camera.aspect = window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight;
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
});
```

Ten thousand cubes floating in a cloud, rendered with a single draw call. On my machine this runs at a steady 60fps. The same scene with 10,000 individual Mesh objects would stutter below 20fps.

The pattern is always the same: create an `Object3D` dummy, set its position/rotation/scale, call `updateMatrix()` to compute the 4x4 transform matrix, then copy it into the InstancedMesh with `setMatrixAt(index, matrix)`. The InstancedMesh stores all transforms in a single buffer internally -- `mesh.instanceMatrix` -- which gets uploaded to the GPU once.

One thing to watch: after modifying instance matrices, you must set `mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true` if you change them after the first render. The initial setup doesn't need this because Three.js uploads the buffer on the first frame automatically.

## Per-instance color

Every instance sharing the same color looks boring. InstancedMesh supports per-instance colors:

```javascript
const coloredMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geo, mat, count);

for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  dummy.position.set(
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40,
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40,
    (Math.random() - 0.5) * 40
  );
  dummy.rotation.set(
    Math.random() * Math.PI,
    Math.random() * Math.PI,
    Math.random() * Math.PI
  );
  dummy.scale.setScalar(0.5 + Math.random() * 1.0);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  coloredMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);

  // per-instance color
  const hue = Math.random();
  coloredMesh.setColorAt(
    i,
    new THREE.Color().setHSL(hue, 0.6, 0.4)
  );
}

// important: mark the color buffer for upload
coloredMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;

scene.add(coloredMesh);
```

`setColorAt(index, color)` stores a per-instance color that multiplies with the material's base color. If the material color is white (0xffffff), the per-instance color becomes the actual color. If the material is blue, the per-instance color tints the blue.

The `instanceColor` buffer works the same as `instanceMatrix` -- it's a single array uploaded to the GPU. After changing any colors, set `coloredMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true`.

## 100,000 cubes: stress test

Let's push it. A hundred thousand cubes arranged in a structured pattern instead of random noise. A cube grid with noise displacement:

```javascript
const gridCount = 100000;
const gridSize = Math.ceil(Math.pow(gridCount, 1/3)); // ~46 per axis
const spacing = 0.6;

const bigGeo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.15, 0.15, 0.15);
const bigMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0xffffff,
  roughness: 0.6
});
const bigMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(bigGeo, bigMat, gridCount);

let idx = 0;
for (let x = 0; x < gridSize && idx < gridCount; x++) {
  for (let y = 0; y < gridSize && idx < gridCount; y++) {
    for (let z = 0; z < gridSize && idx < gridCount; z++) {
      const px = (x - gridSize / 2) * spacing;
      const py = (y - gridSize / 2) * spacing;
      const pz = (z - gridSize / 2) * spacing;

      // noise-based displacement
      const nx = Math.sin(px * 0.3 + py * 0.5) * Math.cos(pz * 0.4) * 0.8;
      const ny = Math.sin(py * 0.4 + pz * 0.3) * Math.cos(px * 0.5) * 0.8;
      const nz = Math.sin(pz * 0.3 + px * 0.4) * Math.cos(py * 0.3) * 0.8;

      dummy.position.set(px + nx, py + ny, pz + nz);
      dummy.rotation.set(nx, ny, nz);
      dummy.scale.setScalar(0.5 + Math.abs(Math.sin(px + py + pz)) * 0.8);
      dummy.updateMatrix();
      bigMesh.setMatrixAt(idx, dummy.matrix);

      // color based on position
      const dist = Math.sqrt(px*px + py*py + pz*pz);
      const hue = dist * 0.03 % 1;
      bigMesh.setColorAt(idx, new THREE.Color().setHSL(hue, 0.5, 0.4));

      idx++;
    }
  }
}

bigMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
scene.add(bigMesh);
```

One hundred thousand individually positioned, colored, scaled cubes. One draw call. This is the power of instancing -- the GPU doesn't care whether it draws 100 instances or 100,000. The geometry is uploaded once. The transforms and colors are just arrays of numbers. The rendering cost scales with total triangle count (which is still low becuase each cube is only 12 triangles), not with the number of separate objects.

On a decent GPU this runs at 60fps. On integrated graphics it might dip to 30-40fps, which is still completely usable. The same count with individual Meshes wouldn't even load -- the browser would run out of memory trying to create 100,000 separate WebGL draw call objects.

## Procedural placement: patterns from algorithms

Random placement is fine for particle clouds, but instancing really shines when you use algorithmic placement. The geometry of the arrangement becomes part of the artwork.

Phyllotaxis -- the spiral pattern you see in sunflower seed heads -- makes a gorgeous 3D layout:

```javascript
const phyllCount = 5000;
const phyllGeo = new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.08, 8, 8);
const phyllMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0xffffff,
  roughness: 0.4,
  metalness: 0.3
});
const phyllMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(phyllGeo, phyllMat, phyllCount);

const goldenAngle = Math.PI * (3 - Math.sqrt(5)); // ~2.3999 radians

for (let i = 0; i < phyllCount; i++) {
  const angle = i * goldenAngle;
  const radius = Math.sqrt(i) * 0.2;
  const height = i * 0.005;

  dummy.position.set(
    Math.cos(angle) * radius,
    height,
    Math.sin(angle) * radius
  );
  dummy.scale.setScalar(0.5 + (i / phyllCount) * 1.5);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  phyllMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);

  const hue = (i / phyllCount) * 0.3 + 0.1;
  phyllMesh.setColorAt(i, new THREE.Color().setHSL(hue, 0.6, 0.45));
}

phyllMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
scene.add(phyllMesh);
```

Five thousand spheres spiraling upward in a phyllotaxis pattern. Each sphere is slightly larger than the last. The colors shift from warm to green as you go up. The golden angle ensures that no two adjacent spheres line up in a straight row -- you get those beautiful interlocking spiral arms that appear in nature.

Other placement algorithms that work well with instancing:

```javascript
// grid with sine wave displacement
for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  const row = Math.floor(i / cols);
  const col = i % cols;
  const x = (col - cols / 2) * spacing;
  const z = (row - rows / 2) * spacing;
  const y = Math.sin(x * 0.5) * Math.cos(z * 0.5) * 2.0;
  // ... set matrix
}

// sphere surface scatter
for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  const phi = Math.acos(2 * Math.random() - 1);
  const theta = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
  const r = 5;
  const x = r * Math.sin(phi) * Math.cos(theta);
  const y = r * Math.sin(phi) * Math.sin(theta);
  const z = r * Math.cos(phi);
  // orient instances to face outward
  dummy.position.set(x, y, z);
  dummy.lookAt(0, 0, 0);
  // ... set matrix
}

// random packing with rejection (no overlaps)
const placed = [];
const minDist = 0.5;
for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  let attempts = 0;
  let ok = false;
  let px, py, pz;
  while (!ok && attempts < 50) {
    px = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 20;
    py = Math.random() * 5;
    pz = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 20;
    ok = true;
    for (const p of placed) {
      const dx = px - p.x, dy = py - p.y, dz = pz - p.z;
      if (dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz < minDist * minDist) {
        ok = false;
        break;
      }
    }
    attempts++;
  }
  if (ok) placed.push({x: px, y: py, z: pz});
  // ... set matrix using px, py, pz
}
```

Each algorithm produces a completely different visual character from the same instanced geometry. The phyllotaxis is organic and mathematical. The sphere scatter creates a shell or planet surface. The rejection packing gives natural-looking distributions without clumping. And they all render at the same speed because instancing doesn't care HOW you compute the transforms -- it just needs the final matrix array.

## Instanced forests

This is where instancing gets practical. A forest of thousands of trees from a single draw call:

```javascript
// simple tree: cone on a cylinder (low-poly stylized)
const trunkGeo = new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.08, 0.12, 0.8, 6);
const trunkMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0x8B6914,
  roughness: 0.9
});
const trunkMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(trunkGeo, trunkMat, 3000);

const crownGeo = new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.5, 1.2, 8);
const crownMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0xffffff,
  roughness: 0.7
});
const crownMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(crownGeo, crownMat, 3000);

const treeCount = 3000;

for (let i = 0; i < treeCount; i++) {
  const x = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 60;
  const z = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 60;
  const scl = 0.6 + Math.random() * 1.4;  // tree size variation
  const rotY = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;

  // trunk
  dummy.position.set(x, scl * 0.4, z);
  dummy.rotation.set(0, rotY, 0);
  dummy.scale.set(scl, scl, scl);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  trunkMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
  trunkMesh.setColorAt(i,
    new THREE.Color().setHSL(0.08, 0.5, 0.15 + Math.random() * 0.1)
  );

  // crown (offset upward relative to trunk)
  dummy.position.set(x, scl * 1.0, z);
  dummy.rotation.set(0, rotY, 0);
  dummy.scale.set(scl, scl, scl);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  crownMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
  crownMesh.setColorAt(i,
    new THREE.Color().setHSL(
      0.25 + Math.random() * 0.1,
      0.4 + Math.random() * 0.2,
      0.2 + Math.random() * 0.15
    )
  );
}

trunkMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
crownMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
scene.add(trunkMesh);
scene.add(crownMesh);
```

Three thousand trees. Two draw calls (one for trunks, one for crowns). Each tree has random position, random size (young trees are small, old ones are large), random rotation so they don't all face the same way, and per-instance color variation so the greens and browns don't look uniform. From a distance this reads as a dense forest. Add fog from ep068 and it looks like a misty woodland.

The trick with multi-part objects (trunk + crown) is to use separate InstancedMesh objects for each part, with matching transforms. The transforms stay synchronized because you compute them from the same seed data. You could even add a third InstancedMesh for fallen leaves at the base of each tree -- same pattern, same indices, just different geometry and offset positions.

## Crystal cave

Another classic instancing scene -- a cave full of glowing crystals:

```javascript
// crystal geometry: elongated octahedron
const crystalGeo = new THREE.OctahedronGeometry(0.15, 0);
// stretch it vertically
const crystalPositions = crystalGeo.attributes.position.array;
for (let i = 0; i < crystalPositions.length; i += 3) {
  crystalPositions[i + 1] *= 3.0; // stretch Y by 3x
}
crystalGeo.computeVertexNormals();

const crystalMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0x000000,
  emissive: 0xffffff,
  emissiveIntensity: 0.8,
  roughness: 0.1,
  metalness: 0.4,
  transparent: true,
  opacity: 0.85
});

const crystalCount = 2000;
const crystalMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(crystalGeo, crystalMat, crystalCount);

for (let i = 0; i < crystalCount; i++) {
  const angle = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
  const r = 3 + Math.random() * 6;
  const x = Math.cos(angle) * r;
  const z = Math.sin(angle) * r;

  // crystals grow from floor and ceiling
  const fromCeiling = Math.random() > 0.6;
  const y = fromCeiling
    ? 5 - Math.random() * 2
    : Math.random() * 0.5;

  const scl = 0.3 + Math.random() * 1.2;

  dummy.position.set(x, y, z);
  // tilt slightly toward center
  dummy.lookAt(0, y, 0);
  dummy.rotation.x += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.4;
  dummy.rotation.z += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.4;
  if (fromCeiling) dummy.rotation.z += Math.PI; // flip upside down
  dummy.scale.set(scl * 0.4, scl, scl * 0.4);
  dummy.updateMatrix();
  crystalMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);

  // color: blues, teals, purples
  const hue = 0.5 + Math.random() * 0.2;
  crystalMesh.setColorAt(i,
    new THREE.Color().setHSL(hue, 0.7, 0.3 + Math.random() * 0.2)
  );
}

crystalMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
scene.add(crystalMesh);

// add a few point lights for that glow effect
const lightColors = [0x4488ff, 0x44ffaa, 0x8844ff];
for (let i = 0; i < lightColors.length; i++) {
  const angle = (i / lightColors.length) * Math.PI * 2;
  const light = new THREE.PointLight(lightColors[i], 2.0, 12);
  light.position.set(Math.cos(angle) * 4, 2, Math.sin(angle) * 4);
  scene.add(light);
}
```

Two thousand crystals -- elongated octahedra growing from the floor and hanging from the ceiling. The geometry was distorted before instancing by scaling the Y coordinates, turning a regular octahedron into a tall crystal shape. Per-instance color in the blue-teal-purple range. Point lights at different positions create that underground glow.

Add bloom from ep069 and the emissive crystals would radiate colored light halos. Even without bloom, the emissive + transparency + metalness gives them a gem-like quality. The point lights make the crystals cast colored light onto each other and the ground.

## Animating instances

Instances can be animated by updating their matrices every frame. The key insight: the matrix buffer is just a Float32Array. Updating it in JavaScript and flagging `needsUpdate` causes the GPU to re-read the transforms. This runs every instance's animation on the CPU, but the rendering is still one draw call.

```javascript
const animCount = 5000;
const animGeo = new THREE.TetrahedronGeometry(0.12, 0);
const animMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0xffffff,
  roughness: 0.3,
  metalness: 0.5
});
const animMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(animGeo, animMat, animCount);

// store initial data for each instance
const particles = [];
for (let i = 0; i < animCount; i++) {
  const angle = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
  const radius = 2 + Math.random() * 6;
  const speed = 0.2 + Math.random() * 0.5;
  const yOffset = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 8;
  const phase = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
  const bobSpeed = 0.5 + Math.random() * 1.5;
  const bobAmount = 0.3 + Math.random() * 0.5;

  particles.push({ angle, radius, speed, yOffset, phase, bobSpeed, bobAmount });

  const hue = angle / (Math.PI * 2);
  animMesh.setColorAt(i, new THREE.Color().setHSL(hue, 0.5, 0.45));
}
animMesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
scene.add(animMesh);

const clock = new THREE.Clock();

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  const t = clock.getElapsedTime();

  for (let i = 0; i < animCount; i++) {
    const p = particles[i];
    const a = p.angle + t * p.speed;

    dummy.position.set(
      Math.cos(a) * p.radius,
      p.yOffset + Math.sin(t * p.bobSpeed + p.phase) * p.bobAmount,
      Math.sin(a) * p.radius
    );
    dummy.rotation.set(t * p.speed, t * p.speed * 0.7, 0);
    dummy.scale.setScalar(0.8 + Math.sin(t * 2 + p.phase) * 0.2);
    dummy.updateMatrix();
    animMesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
  }

  animMesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;

  controls.update();
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}

animate();
```

Five thousand tetrahedra orbiting in a swarm, each with its own speed, radius, bob frequency, and phase. Every frame we loop through all 5000, compute new transforms, and update the matrix buffer. `instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true` tells Three.js to re-upload the buffer to the GPU.

This is where you hit a performance tradeoff. The rendering is fast (one draw call), but the JavaScript loop over 5000 instances every frame costs CPU time. At 5000 instances the loop is barely noticeable. At 50,000 it starts eating into your frame budget. At 500,000 the CPU loop alone takes longer than 16ms and you drop below 60fps even though the GPU is idle.

For massive animated instance counts, the solution is to move the animation into the vertex shader -- but that requires custom per-instance data via `InstancedBufferAttribute`, which we'll get to in a moment.

## Custom per-instance attributes

`setColorAt` and `setMatrixAt` cover transforms and color. But what if you want per-instance animation phase, size multiplier, material variation, or any other custom data? `InstancedBufferAttribute` lets you attach arbitrary per-instance data to the geometry:

```javascript
const customCount = 8000;
const customGeo = new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.1, 8, 8);

// per-instance data: phase, speed, radius
const phases = new Float32Array(customCount);
const speeds = new Float32Array(customCount);
const radii = new Float32Array(customCount);

for (let i = 0; i < customCount; i++) {
  phases[i] = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
  speeds[i] = 0.3 + Math.random() * 0.8;
  radii[i] = 2 + Math.random() * 5;
}

customGeo.setAttribute('aPhase',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(phases, 1)
);
customGeo.setAttribute('aSpeed',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(speeds, 1)
);
customGeo.setAttribute('aRadius',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(radii, 1)
);

// custom shader that reads per-instance attributes
const customMat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
  vertexShader: `
    attribute float aPhase;
    attribute float aSpeed;
    attribute float aRadius;
    uniform float uTime;

    varying vec3 vColor;

    void main() {
      // compute instance position from attributes + time
      float angle = aPhase + uTime * aSpeed;
      vec3 instancePos = vec3(
        cos(angle) * aRadius,
        sin(uTime * aSpeed * 0.5 + aPhase) * 2.0,
        sin(angle) * aRadius
      );

      // color from angle
      float hue = aPhase / 6.283;
      vColor = vec3(
        abs(sin(hue * 6.283)) * 0.5 + 0.3,
        abs(sin(hue * 6.283 + 2.094)) * 0.5 + 0.2,
        abs(sin(hue * 6.283 + 4.188)) * 0.5 + 0.3
      );

      vec3 transformed = position + instancePos;
      gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(transformed, 1.0);
    }
  `,
  fragmentShader: `
    varying vec3 vColor;
    void main() {
      gl_FragColor = vec4(vColor, 1.0);
    }
  `,
  uniforms: {
    uTime: { value: 0 }
  }
});

const customMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(customGeo, customMat, customCount);

// still need to set identity matrices (the shader handles positioning)
const identity = new THREE.Matrix4();
for (let i = 0; i < customCount; i++) {
  customMesh.setMatrixAt(i, identity);
}

scene.add(customMesh);

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  const t = clock.getElapsedTime();

  customMat.uniforms.uTime.value = t;

  controls.update();
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
```

Now the animation runs entirely on the GPU. The per-instance attributes (phase, speed, radius) are uploaded once. The vertex shader reads them and computes each instance's position from those attributes plus the `uTime` uniform. No JavaScript loop per frame, no matrix updates, no `needsUpdate` flag. The CPU just updates one uniform (uTime) and calls render. Eight thousand animated instances for essentially zero CPU cost.

The tradeoff: you need a custom shader. No more `MeshStandardMaterial` with its automatic lighting, shadows, and PBR. You'd have to implement lighting yourself in the fragment shader if you want it. For many creative coding pieces that's fine -- flat colored or emissive particles don't need PBR lighting. For objects that need realistic shading, you can modify Three.js's built-in shaders using `onBeforeCompile`, but that's a more advanced technique.

## Creative exercise: grass field with wind

Allez, time for the big one. Two hundred thousand instanced grass blades on a terrain, with per-instance variation and a vertex shader wind animation. This is the kind of scene you see in game engine demos -- and we're doing it in the browser with raw Three.js:

```javascript
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x88aacc);
scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x88aacc, 0.015);

const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(
  60, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 200
);
camera.position.set(0, 8, 20);

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
renderer.setPixelRatio(window.devicePixelRatio);
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

const controls = new OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);
controls.enableDamping = true;

// lighting
scene.add(new THREE.HemisphereLight(0x88bbdd, 0x445522, 1.5));
const sun = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xfff0dd, 2.0);
sun.position.set(5, 10, 3);
scene.add(sun);

// ground plane
const ground = new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.PlaneGeometry(80, 80),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x3a5a28, roughness: 0.95 })
);
ground.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
scene.add(ground);

// grass blade geometry: a simple triangle
// three vertices: bottom-left, bottom-right, tip
const bladeGeo = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
const bladeVerts = new Float32Array([
  -0.04, 0, 0,   // bottom left
   0.04, 0, 0,   // bottom right
   0,    1, 0    // tip
]);
const bladeNormals = new Float32Array([
  0, 0, 1,
  0, 0, 1,
  0, 0, 1
]);
const bladeUVs = new Float32Array([
  0, 0,
  1, 0,
  0.5, 1
]);
bladeGeo.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(bladeVerts, 3));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('normal', new THREE.BufferAttribute(bladeNormals, 3));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('uv', new THREE.BufferAttribute(bladeUVs, 2));

const grassCount = 200000;

// per-instance attributes
const offsets = new Float32Array(grassCount * 2);    // XZ position
const heights = new Float32Array(grassCount);        // blade height
const leans = new Float32Array(grassCount);           // lean angle
const phases = new Float32Array(grassCount);          // wind phase
const colors = new Float32Array(grassCount * 3);      // RGB tint

for (let i = 0; i < grassCount; i++) {
  offsets[i * 2]     = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 60;
  offsets[i * 2 + 1] = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 60;
  heights[i] = 0.4 + Math.random() * 0.8;
  leans[i] = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.3;
  phases[i] = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;

  // green variation
  const g = 0.25 + Math.random() * 0.2;
  colors[i * 3]     = 0.15 + Math.random() * 0.1;
  colors[i * 3 + 1] = g;
  colors[i * 3 + 2] = 0.05 + Math.random() * 0.08;
}

bladeGeo.setAttribute('aOffset',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(offsets, 2));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('aHeight',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(heights, 1));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('aLean',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(leans, 1));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('aPhase',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(phases, 1));
bladeGeo.setAttribute('aColor',
  new THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(colors, 3));

const grassMat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
  vertexShader: `
    attribute vec2 aOffset;
    attribute float aHeight;
    attribute float aLean;
    attribute float aPhase;
    attribute vec3 aColor;

    uniform float uTime;
    uniform vec2 uWindDir;

    varying vec3 vColor;
    varying float vY;

    void main() {
      vec3 pos = position;

      // scale blade height
      pos.y *= aHeight;
      vY = pos.y / aHeight;

      // lean
      pos.x += pos.y * sin(aLean);

      // wind: bends the blade based on height (tip moves most)
      float windStrength = sin(
        aOffset.x * 0.05 + aOffset.y * 0.03 + uTime * 1.5 + aPhase
      ) * 0.3;
      windStrength += sin(
        aOffset.x * 0.12 - aOffset.y * 0.08 + uTime * 2.3
      ) * 0.1;

      // apply wind as displacement that increases with height
      float heightFactor = pos.y * pos.y;  // quadratic bend
      pos.x += windStrength * heightFactor * uWindDir.x;
      pos.z += windStrength * heightFactor * uWindDir.y;

      // world position
      pos.x += aOffset.x;
      pos.z += aOffset.y;

      // color: darker at base, lighter at tip
      vColor = aColor * (0.6 + vY * 0.4);

      gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0);
    }
  `,
  fragmentShader: `
    varying vec3 vColor;
    varying float vY;

    void main() {
      // slight yellowing at the tip
      vec3 col = vColor;
      col.r += vY * 0.08;
      col.g += vY * 0.03;

      gl_FragColor = vec4(col, 1.0);
    }
  `,
  uniforms: {
    uTime: { value: 0 },
    uWindDir: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1.0, 0.3) }
  },
  side: THREE.DoubleSide
});

const grassMesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(bladeGeo, grassMat, grassCount);

// identity matrices (shader handles positioning)
const identity = new THREE.Matrix4();
for (let i = 0; i < grassCount; i++) {
  grassMesh.setMatrixAt(i, identity);
}

scene.add(grassMesh);

const clock = new THREE.Clock();

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  const t = clock.getElapsedTime();

  grassMat.uniforms.uTime.value = t;

  controls.update();
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}

animate();

window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  camera.aspect = window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight;
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
});
```

Two hundred thousand grass blades. Each one a single triangle -- three vertices, that's it. The geometry is tiny. The instancing makes the count irrelevant to draw call overhead. The vertex shader handles everything: positioning each blade at its XZ offset, scaling to its height, applying a lean angle, and then the wind.

The wind is two layered sine waves that use the blade's world position as input. This means the wind isn't random per-blade -- it's a spatial wave that propagates across the field. Blades near each other sway similarly. Blades far apart sway out of phase. The wave rolls across the field like a real wind gust, creating those visible ripples you see in a meadow on a breezy day.

The quadratic height factor (`pos.y * pos.y`) means the base of each blade barely moves while the tip sways widely. That's how real grass bends -- anchored at the root, flexible at the tip. Linear height factor would make the whole blade lean uniformly, which looks stiff and wrong.

The color gradient (darker base, lighter yellowish tip) adds a subtle touch of realism. Real grass is yellowish at the tips where it dries out and deep green at the base where it's healthy. Small detail, big visual difference.

From the right camera angle -- low, looking across the field at sunset lighting -- this looks surprisingly photorealistic. Two hundred thousand triangles with a simple shader, and it passes for a field of grass. Instancing makes the impossible possible in the browser.

## Performance guide

A few numbers from testing, so you know what to expect:

**Instance count vs frame rate** (rough guideline, modern laptop GPU):
- 1,000-10,000: no measurable overhead, max fps
- 10,000-100,000: still 60fps for simple geometry, might dip on mobile
- 100,000-500,000: 30-60fps depending on geometry complexity and shader cost
- 500,000-1,000,000: possible but you need very simple geometry (one triangle per instance) and a lightweight shader

**CPU-animated vs GPU-animated instances**:
- CPU loop (updating matrices in JS): practical up to about 10,000-20,000 instances per frame. Beyond that the loop itself consumes too much frame budget
- GPU animated (vertex shader + InstancedBufferAttribute): scales to hundreds of thousands because the CPU only updates uniforms, the GPU does all per-instance work in parallel

**Geometry complexity per instance**: instancing doesn't magically eliminate triangle processing. 100,000 instances of a 100-triangle cube = 10 million triangles per frame. That's fine. 100,000 instances of a 10,000-triangle model = 1 billion triangles. That's not fine. Keep per-instance geometry simple -- the visual richness comes from quantity and variation, not indvidual detail.

**Memory**: each instance needs a 4x4 float matrix (64 bytes) plus optional color (12 bytes) plus any custom attributes. For 1 million instances with color and two custom floats: ~84MB. That's significant. Be mindful of mobile device memory limits.

## What's ahead

We covered instancing from basics to advanced: InstancedMesh for rendering thousands of identical objects in one draw call, per-instance transforms via Matrix4, per-instance color with setColorAt, procedural placement algorithms (phyllotaxis, sphere scatter, rejection packing), multi-part instanced objects for forests, animated instances with CPU loops and GPU vertex shaders, custom per-instance attributes with InstancedBufferAttribute, and a full grass field with wind simulation.

Next time we'll shape text in 3D space -- extruded letters, text along curves, particle text effects. Typography as geometry opens up a whole different visual vocabulary for creative coding, and combined with instancing and post-processing, the possibilities are kind of ridiculous.

## 't Komt erop neer...

- InstancedMesh renders N copies of one geometry+material in a single draw call. Constructor takes `(geometry, material, count)`. Set per-instance transforms with `setMatrixAt(index, matrix)` using an Object3D dummy: set position/rotation/scale, call `updateMatrix()`, copy the matrix. After modifying matrices post-first-render, set `mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true`
- Per-instance color with `setColorAt(index, color)` -- multiplies with the material base color. Set `mesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true` after changes. Colors use a single buffer array on the GPU, same pattern as the matrix buffer
- 100,000 instances at 60fps is routine on modern hardware. The GPU doesn't care about instance count -- it cares about total triangle count and shader complexity. Keep per-instance geometry simple (a few triangles to a few hundred) and the numbers scale massively
- Procedural placement algorithms produce distinctive visual patterns: phyllotaxis spirals (golden angle), sphere surface scatter, grid with noise displacement, rejection-based random packing. The placement math is separate from the instancing -- compute positions however you want, then feed them as matrices
- Multi-part instanced objects (forest trees = trunk InstancedMesh + crown InstancedMesh) share the same index mapping. Compute transforms from the same seed data so parts stay aligned. Two draw calls for a 3000-tree forest
- CPU-animated instances: update matrices in a JavaScript loop each frame, flag `needsUpdate`. Practical up to ~20,000 instances. Beyond that the loop eats too much frame budget
- GPU-animated instances: attach per-instance data via `InstancedBufferAttribute` on the geometry, read it in a custom vertex shader. Animation runs entirely on the GPU in parallel. Scales to hundreds of thousands of instances with near-zero CPU cost. Tradeoff: you lose MeshStandardMaterial's automatic lighting (unless you reimplement it or use `onBeforeCompile`)
- The grass field recipe: one-triangle blade geometry, per-instance attributes for offset/height/lean/phase/color, vertex shader wind as spatial sine waves that use world position (creates propagating wave across the field, not random per-blade jitter), quadratic height factor for realistic bending (tip moves most, base stays anchored)

Sallukes! Thanks for reading.

X

@femdev

